-- Reduction Erdos1052.erdos_1052.R1.H2.R1.H1.R1.H1.R1.H3.R1.H1.R2 of step
-- Erdos1052.erdos_1052.R1.H2.R1.H1.R1.H1.R1.H3.R1.H1
-- Splits the open step (3 the only repeated odd prime, e = n.factorization 3 > 500, e no odd
-- multiple of any d in the first list, a no odd multiple of any d in the second list) by the
-- residue of e modulo 4, which the step leaves free.  The residue decides v_2(3^e + 1) and the
-- small primes dividing 3^e + 1:
--   e = 0 mod 4: 3^e = 1 mod 8, so v_2(3^e+1) = 1, 5 does not divide 3^e+1, 3^e+1 = 2 * odd;
--   e = 1 mod 4: e odd, v_2(3^e+1) = 2, 3^e = 3 mod 8;
--   e = 2 mod 4: v_2(3^e+1) = 1 and 5 | 3^e+1 (3^2 = -1 mod 5), so 5 is a simple prime of n
--                with source 3^e+1: a constraint absent from the other three classes;
--   e = 3 mod 4: e odd, v_2(3^e+1) = 2, and 7 | 3^e+1 exactly when e = 3 mod 6 (e = 3 mod 12 here).
-- The 2-adic part of the balance equation (2^a+1)(3^e+1) prod (q+1) = 2^(a+1) 3^e prod q is
-- a + 1 = v_2(3^e+1) + sum_q v_2(q+1), so the four classes have different parity bookkeeping for a
-- and force different small primes into n.  Proving the four classes closes the step.

